<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramacharita</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ramacharita is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> poem written in the twelfth century by Sandhyakara Nandi, in which each verse can be read in two ways: as the story of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> and as the history of the Pala king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramapala">Ramapala</a>. It is the main source for the revolt of the Kaivartas in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and for Ramapala's recovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varendra">Varendra</a> with the help of allied chiefs, whom it names.</p></div>
